description:
File: crate-jmap-chat-server/src/backend.rs:563-605

  /// Predicate consulted by typing/presence fan-out paths: is the
  /// requesting caller blocked by the given contact?
  ///
  /// ...when the requesting account corresponds to a [ChatContact]
  /// whose blocked is true on the recipient's contact list, the
  /// server MUST silently suppress the ephemeral event...
  fn is_contact_blocked(
      &self,
      _caller: &Self::CallerCtx,
      _account_id: &jmap_types::Id,
      _contact_id: &jmap_types::Id,
  ) -> impl Future<Output = Result<bool, Self::Error>> + Send;

The trait doc and the argument list disagree about whose
perspective `account_id` represents:

- The first sentence says 'is the requesting caller blocked by
  the given contact?' — so account_id is the caller's account,
  and contact_id refers to the blocker.

- The next sentence says 'when the requesting account corresponds
  to a ChatContact whose blocked is true on the recipient's
  contact list' — so the contact list to consult belongs to the
  RECIPIENT, not the caller. Which account's contact list does
  account_id refer to?

JMAP-x2gd.16 (already filed) flags the related implementation
bug: MemoryBackend.is_contact_blocked uses get_objects(account_id)
but ChatContact.id is global identity — which is exactly the
symptom of an unclear contract.

The kit's caller-identity foundation (workspace AGENTS.md 'Caller
identity (foundation seam)') gives the right vocabulary to write
this method's contract clearly:

  /// Returns true when the sender corresponds to a ChatContact
  /// (identified by caller's principal_id) whose `blocked` field
  /// is `true` on the contact list owned by the account identified
  /// by `account_id` (the recipient of the ephemeral event).
  ///
  /// The contact list to consult is the RECIPIENT's, not the
  /// caller's. `contact_id` is the ChatContact id WITHIN the
  /// recipient's account that corresponds to the sender.

That re-phrasing makes the data-flow direction explicit:
'recipient's account_id' is the OWNER of the contact list, and
'caller's identity' is the SUBJECT being checked for the blocked
flag.

Compounding gap: the doc does not say what
'account_id corresponds to a ChatContact' means when
JmapBackend::principal_id returns None (single-user mode). The
default impl returns Ok(false), so it doesn't matter for the
reference impl; but a production backend that wires identity
needs to know whether to fail-safe (treat None as unknown sender,
block conservatively) or fail-open (treat None as not-blocked).

Fix: rewrite the rustdoc to explicitly name each arg's role
('account_id = contact-list owner', 'contact_id = candidate
ChatContact in that list', 'caller = identity to compare against
the ChatContact's principal binding'). Optionally rename
arguments to reflect their role:
  fn is_contact_blocked(
      &self,
      sender: &Self::CallerCtx,
      list_owner_account_id: &jmap_types::Id,
      contact_id: &jmap_types::Id,
  ) -> ...
